{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>I will give the land.</b> The doubling of expressions in the Torah is not for nothing. And it is possible by way of the Kabbalah that 'the land' mentioned at the beginning of the verse is the attribute through which the three patriarchs were blessed — בכל מכל כל (all, from all, everything). And it says 'I have given,' 'I will give it to you,' in the manner of what is written (I Kings 5:26): ״וה׳ נתן חכמה לשלמה״ — 'and HaShem gave wisdom to Solomon' — and therefore He went back and repeated 'I will give the land,' meaning the land of Israel that He will give to his seed. And so He did with the second verse: ״ויעל מעליו אלהים במקום אשר דבר אתו״ — 'and Elohim went up from him in the place where He spoke with him' — which speaks also of above, meaning 'in the place of the One speaking with him.' And so the Ramban, z\"l, wrote; his words: By way of the truth, 'Elohim went up from him in the place of the One speaking with him,' in the manner of what is said (Ezekiel 3:12): ״ברוך כבוד ה׳ ממקומו״ — 'Blessed be the glory of HaShem from His place.' And he said: what the Sages mentioned, 'the patriarchs, they are the Merkavah.' End of quote. Therefore the verse that follows it comes."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The Ramban quotation includes 'ואמר' leading into the chain; the trailing 'ע\"כ' closes the Ramban citation. The phrase 'the patriarchs, they are the Merkavah' is a well-known rabbinic dictum; 'ע\"כ' = עד כאן, 'end of quote.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:50.377406911Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}